1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,206
For 30 years, the smartest investors on Wall Street handed him their money.

2
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:06,616
Nobody questioned him.

3
00:00:06,616 --> 00:00:08,089
Nobody asked for proof.

4
00:00:08,089 --> 00:00:12,575
Because the man running the scam used to run the stock market himself.

5
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:16,003
His name opened doors nobody else could open.

6
00:00:16,003 --> 00:00:18,013
He served as chairman of NASDAQ.

7
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:19,458
Regulators trusted him.

8
00:00:19,458 --> 00:00:20,589
Banks trusted him.

9
00:00:20,589 --> 00:00:24,925
Investors didn't just trust him, they begged him to take their money.

10
00:00:25,525 --> 00:00:28,205
In reality, there were red flags for years.

11
00:00:28,205 --> 00:00:33,815
His returns were impossibly consistent, up every single month, even during market crashes.

12
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:38,240
One financial analyst tried to warn the SEC in writing, multiple times.

13
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:39,175
He was ignored.

14
00:00:39,775 --> 00:00:42,141
It wasn't regulators who caught him.

15
00:00:42,141 --> 00:00:44,310
It was the 2008 financial crisis.

16
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:50,027
When investors panicked and asked for their money back, there was nothing left to give.

17
00:00:50,027 --> 00:00:53,116
The fund was never actually investing anything.

18
00:00:53,116 --> 00:00:57,125
It was simply paying old investors with new investors' money.

19
00:00:57,725 --> 00:01:03,186
This is now known as the largest Ponzi scheme in history, an estimated $65 billion.

20
00:01:03,186 --> 00:01:05,225
But here's the unsettling part.

21
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:11,279
The system designed to catch fraud like this had every opportunity to stop it years earlier.

22
00:01:11,279 --> 00:01:11,936
It didn't.

23
00:01:11,936 --> 00:01:15,292
So the real question isn't how he got away with it.

24
00:01:15,292 --> 00:01:18,450
It's: how many more are still running right now?
